# Week 1 — Initial Discovery & Project Context

## 1. Facts
People sign up using emails, phone calls, paper forms, or spreadsheets instead of one central place
Event organisers spend hours matching the right volunteers to the right tasks by hand
Volunteers often do not know which events actually need help
It is hard to let everyone know quickly when an event schedule changes or gets cancelled
Some tasks need specific skills, background checks, or qualifications (e.g., Garda vetting/first aid)

## 2. Assumptions
Organisers and administrative staff have reliable internet access and web browsers during operational hours
Volunteers prefer automated digital notifications (email/SMS/app push) over phone calls for routine updates
Each event or activity role has a defined maximum capacity of volunteers it can accept
Volunteers have basic digital literacy to create an account and view event schedules online

## 3. Unknowns
How are volunteer qualifications, certifications, and background checks (e.g., Garda vetting) currently verified and who signs off on them
Who holds the exact authority to create, modify, or cancel a community event or specific volunteer role
What specific data retention and privacy restrictions apply to sharing volunteer contact details with team leaders
What existing external calendar or identity systems (if any) need to integrate with the new platform

## 4. Stakeholders
Event Organiser: Needs automated matching tools and quick group communication. Concern: Last-minute volunteer cancellations leaving key roles open
General Volunteer: Needs clear schedule visibility, easy sign-up, and timely notifications. Concern: Missing schedule changes or receiving irrelevant updates
Skilled Volunteer: Needs role-specific assignments matching verified qualifications (e.g., first aid, heavy machinery license). Concern: Being assigned to generic/unqualified tasks
IT Support / System Administrator: Needs simple user management, role-based access control, and low system maintenance. Concern: Security vulnerabilities and system downtime
Privacy / Data Protection Officer: Needs strict compliance with GDPR, explicit consent tracking, and data minimization. Concern: Unauthorized access to volunteers' personal contact details
Community Members: Need reliable event execution and clear public communication. Concern: Poorly organized events or cancelled activities without notice
Sponsors / Funders: Need clear impact reports showing volunteer engagement statistics. Concern: Lack of transparency or misuse of funding resources

## 5. Goals
Goal 1 Efficiency: Reduce the manual administrative time spent matching volunteers to events by at least 60%.
Goal 2 Communication: Eliminate double-bookings and ensure 100% of registered volunteers receive automated notifications within 5 minutes of an event cancellation or schedule update
Goal 3 Compliance & Quality: Prevent unqualified or unvetted volunteers from being assigned to restricted or specialized event roles

## 6. Reflection
Starting with a problem-first approach rather than jumping straight into proposing features (like an "iOS app" or "AI matching algorithm") made it clear that the core issue is process coordination and verified tracking, not just UI design. Categorizing facts separately from assumptions prevented making unverified technical decisions early on. Identifying non-user stakeholders like the Privacy Officer and Sponsors brought crucial GDPR and reporting requirements to light before designing any database schema or interface mockup.
